Requeue a ProxmoxCluster that is marked for deletion before its Cluster.

Upstream's reconcileDelete returns without a requeue while the owning Cluster has no deletion
timestamp, and counts on the Cluster's deletion to trigger it again. The Cluster watch uses
predicates.ClusterUnpaused, which passes only creates and unpauses, and capmox sets no SyncPeriod.
So the ProxmoxCluster is next reconciled at controller-runtime's resync, about 10 hours later, and
a namespace delete that marks the ProxmoxCluster first hangs until then.

The fix requeues after infrav1.DefaultReconcilerRequeue, as the machines-left branch of the same
function does. The wait is logged as info, since it repeats every 10 seconds for a ProxmoxCluster
deleted by hand while its Cluster lives. A watch that also passes a deleting Cluster would change
the predicate every reconcile path shares.

diff --git a/internal/controller/proxmoxcluster_controller.go b/internal/controller/proxmoxcluster_controller.go
index 7e48377..2db29dd 100644
--- a/internal/controller/proxmoxcluster_controller.go
+++ b/internal/controller/proxmoxcluster_controller.go
@@ -156,9 +156,9 @@ func (r *ProxmoxClusterReconciler) Reconcile(ctx context.Context, req ctrl.Reque
 func (r *ProxmoxClusterReconciler) reconcileDelete(ctx context.Context, clusterScope *scope.ClusterScope) (reconcile.Result, error) {
 	// We want to prevent deletion unless the owning cluster was flagged for deletion.
 	if clusterScope.Cluster.DeletionTimestamp.IsZero() {
-		clusterScope.Error(errors.New("deletion was requested but owning cluster wasn't deleted"), "Unable to delete ProxmoxCluster")
-		// We stop reconciling here. It will be triggered again once the owning cluster was deleted.
-		return reconcile.Result{}, nil
+		clusterScope.Info("waiting for the owning cluster to be deleted")
+		// The Cluster watch does not pass a Cluster being deleted, so poll.
+		return ctrl.Result{RequeueAfter: infrav1.DefaultReconcilerRequeue}, nil
 	}

 	clusterScope.Logger.V(4).Info("Reconciling ProxmoxCluster delete")
